# pseudocron.env.example — Example environment file for pseudocron.py
#
# install-pseudocron.sh installs this file automatically when pseudocron.env
# does not already exist.  To install both pseudocron.cfg and pseudocron.env
# in one step, run:
#
#   team/scripts/install-pseudocron.sh --wake-tier=small   # (default)
#   team/scripts/install-pseudocron.sh --wake-tier=medium
#   team/scripts/install-pseudocron.sh --wake-tier=large
#
# To copy and edit this file manually:
#
#   cp team/scripts/pseudocron.env.example "$PGAI_AGENT_KANBAN_ROOT_PATH/pseudocron.env"
#   $EDITOR "$PGAI_AGENT_KANBAN_ROOT_PATH/pseudocron.env"
#
# Note: an existing pseudocron.env is NEVER overwritten by install-pseudocron.sh
# (it may contain operator customizations).  Delete the file and re-run
# install-pseudocron.sh to regenerate from this example.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# PURPOSE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every job pseudocron starts gets pseudocron.py's own environment —
# inherited from whatever launched it (a shell, a systemd unit, the
# container entrypoint) — with this file's variables laid over it. A name
# set here replaces the inherited value for the jobs; it never changes
# pseudocron.py's own environment.
#
# If this file does not exist, jobs get the inherited environment
# unchanged and pseudocron logs a notice to stderr. That is not an error.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   export NAME=VALUE       — standard bash export form (preferred)
#   NAME=VALUE              — bare assignment (also accepted)
#   # comment               — ignored
#   <blank line>            — ignored
#
# Supported value styles:
#   export FOO=plain_value
#   export FOO="value with spaces"
#   export FOO='value with spaces'
#
# NOT supported (no shell expansion):
#   export FOO=$(command)   — command substitution
#   export FOO=${BAR:-default}  — parameter expansion
#   If you need these, pre-expand them in a real shell and paste the
#   literal values here.
#
# Parser behaviour to know:
#   - A line that is not one of the shapes above is skipped silently (no
#     warning) — check spelling if a variable seems not to arrive.
#   - Everything after the '=' is the value, so a trailing comment is part
#     of it: `export FOO=bar  # note` sets FOO to "bar  # note". Put
#     comments on their own line.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# VARIABLES — uncomment and adjust as needed
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Kanban root ---
# PGAI_AGENT_KANBAN_ROOT_PATH must already be set in the environment
# pseudocron.py is LAUNCHED from: pseudocron.py reads it to find this file
# and pseudocron.cfg, and exits 1 naming it when it is unset. Setting it
# here does nothing for the scheduler itself; jobs already inherit the
# launch value. Leave it out of this file.
# No default (required in the launch environment).

# --- PATH — what the jobs search for binaries ---
# Default: inherited from the launching environment.
# A PATH set here REPLACES the inherited one (no $PATH expansion happens),
# so it must list everything the jobs need: a python3 of version 3.12+ and
# the claude CLI (and the codex CLI if that provider is active).
# export PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# --- HOME — set if pseudocron is run by a system init (no home dir) ---
# Default: inherited from the launching environment.
# The claude and codex CLIs read their credentials under $HOME.
# export HOME=/srv/kanban

# --- Per-role model overrides ---
# PGAI_<ROLE>_MODEL for ROLE in PM, CODER, WRITER, TESTER, CM, PO picks the
# model that role's wakes run with. Read by the wake's config loader
# (scripts/lib/config_loader.sh).
# Default: unset, and the model comes from kanban.cfg
# [models.<provider>] <role>, then [models.<provider>] default, then the
# provider CLI's own default.
# export PGAI_CODER_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5
# export PGAI_PO_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5

# Credentials do not belong in this file: it is neither "secrets" nor kept
# at mode 0600. Wakes read credentials from the CLI's own login (the
# default oauth mode needs no variable) or from the secrets file — see
# secrets_example.

# --- Variables of your own (illustrative only) ---
# Any NAME=VALUE line reaches every job. These names are examples, not
# variables the kanban reads.
# export MY_VAR=some_value

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Add your variables below this line.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
